---
import BaseLayout from '../../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Sermorelin: Status, History & Real Risks — Nawtch"
  description="Sermorelin's FDA history (once approved, now discontinued), why it's not approved for fitness use today, and what research exists."
  canonicalPath="/supplements/peptides/sermorelin/"
  bodyClass="diets"
>
      <header>
        <div class="header-left">
          <h1>Sermorelin</h1>
          <p class="subtitle">Once FDA-approved and discontinued for business reasons, not safety — now compounding-only.</p>
        </div>
        <a class="ghost-btn" href="/supplements/peptides/">← Back to Peptides</a>
      </header>

      <div class="settings-section" style="border:1px solid var(--accent); background:rgba(200,240,96,0.08);">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title" style="color:var(--accent);">Last Verified: August 14, 2026</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section" style="border:1px solid var(--accent3); background:rgba(240,160,96,0.08);">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title" style="color:var(--accent3);">Tier 2 — Investigational / Not Currently Approved</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            Sermorelin has real medical history, but is <strong>not currently FDA-approved</strong> for any use, and current fitness/anti-aging use has little modern human trial support.
            This page describes its history, mechanism, and known risks — <strong>not dosing or usage guidance, and not a recommendation to use it.</strong> Talk to a doctor before considering it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title">What It Is</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            Sermorelin is a synthetic peptide analog of the first 29 amino acids of human GHRH
            (growth hormone-releasing hormone). Rather than supplying growth hormone directly the
            way injectable HGH does, it binds GHRH receptors on the pituitary gland and stimulates
            the body's own GH release — working through the natural feedback loop instead of
            overriding it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title">FDA History</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            Sermorelin was FDA-approved under the brand name <strong>Geref</strong> — first in
            1990 for diagnostic testing of pituitary function, then in 1997 for treating pediatric
            growth hormone deficiency. In 2008 the manufacturer, EMD Serono, discontinued the
            product and asked the FDA to withdraw both approvals; the withdrawal took effect in
            2009. Notably, the FDA's own Federal Register determination explicitly states Geref
            was <strong>not withdrawn for reasons of safety or effectiveness</strong> — it was a
            business decision by the manufacturer.
          </p>
          <br />
          <a href="https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2013/03/04/2013-04827/determination-that-geref-sermorelin-acetate-injection-05-milligrams-basevial-and-10-milligrams" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Federal Register — FDA determination on Geref withdrawal →</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title">Current Status &amp; What Research Exists</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            Sermorelin is not currently FDA-approved or commercially marketed as an approved drug.
            It's available today only through compounding pharmacies, by prescription, typically
            used off-label for adult GH-boosting, anti-aging, or fitness purposes — a different
            use case from its original, narrow, disease-specific approvals. The human trial data
            behind those original approvals is decades old and specific to diagnostic testing and
            pediatric GH deficiency; there isn't robust modern clinical trial data supporting its
            popular off-label uses in healthy adults.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title">Known Risks</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            Reported side effects include injection-site reactions, transient flushing, headache,
            nausea, and an unusual taste sensation. There's also a theoretical concern about
            effects on insulin sensitivity and glucose metabolism with GH-elevating compounds
            generally. It's generally considered to carry a different risk profile than exogenous
            HGH, since it works through the body's own feedback regulation rather than overriding
            it — but that isn't the same as having a modern safety record for off-label,
            long-term adult use.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title">Learn More</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <a href="https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2013/03/04/2013-04827/determination-that-geref-sermorelin-acetate-injection-05-milligrams-basevial-and-10-milligrams" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Federal Register — Determination on Geref (Sermorelin) Withdrawal</a>
        </div>
      </div>
</BaseLayout>
